---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/**
 * Written from docs/PRIVACY-ARCHITECTURE.md in the app repo, which describes
 * what the code actually does. Not from intentions.
 *
 * The old Nudgy site's privacy page said the product "runs entirely on your Mac"
 * and that nothing left the device. That was true of a local screenshot tool and
 * became false the moment anything synced. This page must never drift the same
 * way — re-read it whenever the app starts storing something new. That's a
 * standing item in the repo's session wrap-up checklist.
 */
const updated = '25 September 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — Nudgy"
  description="What Nudgy stores, what it deliberately doesn't, and what we can and can't see."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <main class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="faint">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p>
      Nudgy holds private notes about people who never signed up for it, can’t log in, and mostly don’t know
      it exists. That shapes every decision below, and it’s why this page tries to be specific rather than
      reassuring.
    </p>

    <h2>What Nudgy stores</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your email address, name, timezone, and reminder preferences.</li>
      <li>The people you add: names, how you know them, and any contact details you type in.</li>
      <li>Notes you write, and things you’ve noted that someone needs.</li>
      <li>That you reached out to someone, when, and by what means.</li>
      <li>Dates you ask us to remember, like birthdays.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Nudgy deliberately doesn’t do</h2>
    <p>Each of these is a decision, not a feature we haven’t got to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It does not import your address book or your Google Contacts. You add people one at a time.</li>
      <li>It does not read your email or your calendar.</li>
      <li>It does not store photos of anyone.</li>
      <li>It does not use your location.</li>
      <li>It does not send your notes to an AI model. There is no AI in Nudgy.</li>
      <li>It has no analytics, no tracking, no session recording, and no third-party SDKs.</li>
      <li>It does not sell or share your information. There is nobody to sell it to.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who can see your data</h2>
    <p>
      Your data is isolated at the database level, not just in the app. Every record is tied to your account,
      and the database itself refuses to return another user’s rows — so a bug in the app can’t leak them.
      That rule is covered by automated tests that run on every change.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What that does not mean:</strong> your notes are stored in a way that we, as the operator of the
      service, could technically read. They’re encrypted on disk, which protects against someone physically
      stealing a drive — it does not make them invisible to us. We don’t read them, and nothing in the product
      does anything with them. But we would rather say that plainly than imply a guarantee we haven’t built.
    </p>
    <p>
      End-to-end encryption, where even we couldn’t read them, is something we want to add. When it exists,
      this page will say so. Until then, it doesn’t.
    </p>

    <h2>Notifications</h2>
    <p>
      Reminders are scheduled on your own device and never sent from a server. A contact’s name never travels
      through Apple’s or Google’s systems — and notifications never contain anyone’s name anyway, since lock
      screens are readable by whoever’s holding the phone. You’ll see “3 people to reach out to”, never a name.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Export.</strong> Settings → Export everything. You get a JSON file with all of it.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delete.</strong> Settings → Delete my account. This permanently removes every person, note,
        and interaction. It can’t be undone, and we can’t recover it for you.
      </li>
      <li>Deleted records are purged within 30 days, including from backups.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you’re in someone’s Nudgy</h2>
    <p>
      Someone may have written notes about you without telling you. You have rights over that information even
      though you never signed up. Email <a href="mailto:privacy@pragmaticlabs.dev">privacy@pragmaticlabs.dev</a>
      and we’ll tell you what we can and remove it on request.
    </p>

    <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
    <p>Nudgy runs on services operated by other companies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Supabase</strong> — the database and sign-in.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — hosting.</li>
      <li><strong>Expo</strong> — building and distributing the apps.</li>
      <li><strong>Apple and Google</strong> — app distribution.</li>
      <li>An email provider, to send your sign-in codes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This list changes only when the product does, and this page changes with it.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Nudgy is made by Pragmatic Labs. Questions, or anything that looks wrong here:
      <a href="mailto:privacy@pragmaticlabs.dev">privacy@pragmaticlabs.dev</a>.
    </p>

    <footer>
      <a href="/">Nudgy</a>
      <a href="/terms">Terms</a>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Base>
